Section A

Explanation on what fixed_storage.py does

from argon2 import PasswordHasher

This imports the PasswordHasher class from the argon2-cffi library, which just handles all the Argon2 operations

from argon2.exceptions import VerifyMismatchError

Imports specific exceptions that Argon2 raises when a password doesnt match the specific hash its comparing it to. Importing this rather
than catching all exceptions later on with raw python code makes sure errors are precise without any user error coming into play.

hasher = PasswordHasher(time_cost=2, memory_cost=65536, parallelism=2)

This creates a hasher instance with hard coded numbers rather than the library defaults it uses. time_cost=2 means only 2 iterations
of the algorithm. memory_cost=65536 means that specifically 64 MB of ram is required to do each has computation which would defeat GPU based
attacks since they have limited memory. parallelism=2 means 2 threads are used. These parameters make each hash expensive on purpose.

def hash_password(password: str)->str:

	return hasher.hash(password)

This calls Argons hash function on the plaintext password that is passed in. The library automatically generates a random salt and
runs the algorithm with the parameters I set then returns a single send-contained string that encodes the algorithm name, parameters,
salt, and hash.

def verify_password(password: str,stored_hash: str)-> bool:

	try 
		hasher.verify(stored_hash, password)
		
		return True
		
		except VerifyMismatchError:

		return False

This Extracts the salt and parameters from stored_hash, re-hashes the candidates password and compares each result. Returns true if they
match and VerifyMismatchError only appears when the hash is corrupted instead of just having it return false.


Q1: I started with a plain dictionary attack using the rockyou.txt since I read it'd be the fastest mode with no other modifications to it.
 Using the rockyou.txt cracked 17 hashes instantly. This gave me the info that most of the passwords were simple words that were
 easy to scan for. 28 more to crack, I started again with the same wordlist with a rules file best64.rule, I thought that it was the best second course of action since common passwords are usually a word or two with a little modification like capitlization, adding
a number or replacing a letter in a word entirely. This cracked 4 more (example of one  Evans123). Then I tried the xato wordlist which
cracked 0 new hashes, which told me the last few passwords werent in any wordlists given or something that would be posted online.
I moved to brute force the remaining passwords using 6-string 7-string and - string vcharacters which cracked 5 more
 (they looked like random strings of text) but didnt crack any more passwords.

Q2: The plain dictionary attack cracked the most passwords with 17 hashes. This tell us that these users chose passwords that are in the
passwords like (simple string passwords like sebastian, jessica, bob) These users went for the easy access of short simple passwords
that're easy to remember. Their passwords were common.

Q3:  The xato wordlist was the least cracked. I targeted mask attacks, and additional rule variations on it and I cracked zero passwords.
The 18 passwords resisted every mode. A password resists when it doesnt have any correlation to any real word or common patterns that may
be used. It goes against brute force when it's long enough for the brute force to take forver (for example a 8-char password has
quadrillions of combinations.) Id like to believe these passwords are longer than 8-characters and just used the most random sequences
with no structure.

Section B

Q4: the failed password crack tells us that the second hash is not MD5 even if it looks identical. MD5 isnt the only hash that outputs
32 characters because NTLM does too. I did a little research for this question and I found that -m wouldnt crach a NTLM hash even if the
password is in the wordlist , since the hash funtionality is different.

The steps I would take is trying the command hashcat -a 0 -m 1000 hash.txt wordlist.txt and if it gets the password immediately it 
confirms that the hash is HTLM. If it fails I would try different variations like  MD4 -m 900 LM (lan manager) -m 3000, double MD5 -m 2600
and if none of these work I would look up a list of algorithms to crack a hash.

Q5: I would try 32-Char hex first, 64-char hex second, and then $2b$12$ last. 

32char MD5  hex strings because MD5 was designed specifically for speed. I know I have limited gpu space so this would be the best choice as
MD5 has no real built in work factor because it just checks a dictionary. 

64-char SHA 256 since its faster than bcrypt but noticably slower than MD5. Its similar having little to do workload on your graphics
card but slower in a password cracking race against MD5

$2b$12$ stirngs in bcrypt
Id attack them last and depending on how much gpu time I have even skip it. bcrypt has a very costly time factor which would make our
card run a ton of iterations per password. 

Section C

Q6: MD5 speed: 4,135.9 MH/s = 4,135,900,000 hashes per second

bcrypt speed: 3,387 H/s = 3,387 hashes per second

Ratio: 4,135,900,000 ÷ 3,387 = around 1,220,900

In one second my machine tested over 4 billion md5 password candidates bbut only 3,387 bcrypt passwords. Thats a huge difference meaning
an attacker could go through an entire dictionary like rockyou.txt against md5 in  under a second whule the same attack using bcrypt
would take more thn an hour on my software or (the same software they're using) which means brute force attempts against bcrypt wouldnt
be worth the longer or more complicated the password is.

Q7: the answer would be around 208.8 billion

Keyspace: 26^8 = 208,827,064,576

MD5:
208,827,064,576 ÷ 4,135,900,000 H/s = 50.5 seconds

bcrypt:
208,827,064,576 ÷ 3,387 H/s = 61,659,734 seconds
÷ 60 = 1,027,662 minutes
÷ 60 = 17,127 hours
÷ 24 = 713 days	

Section D
 
Q8: Without cracking anything you can say that both the users have the same password. Since the hash is unsalted, identical input will
always have the same output. If an attacker could crack one password they would know what the other password is if their accounts use
the same password without any extra work. For an organization storing an unsalted table means that its one hash away from having every
account taken and compromised.

Q8 P2: A rainbow table attack works aainst unsalted.txt because the attackers system looks through a massive table of hash password pairs.
Cracking them just becomes a simple lookup of finding the same hash in the table and reading out the password. It would fail against salted because the has is computed the same way but with a unique salt per user. For this attack the attacker would have to compute one table but also another table for the salt for EVERY user. which computationally is a lot for your storage. It wouldnt make the 
hashes harder to crack but only worthless because the new tables you're making are useless against any other salt you're attacking.

Q9: 

Yes the salt still defeats rainbow tables because even though the attacker has both the hash and the salt, rainbow tables are still
unuseable. Rainbow tables only work without knowing the salt in advance (without a salt). Since every row has a different salt, the
attacker cant use a generic table against any of them. 

The salt doesnt slow down a local hashcat that much. With both the hash and salt, hashcat computes md5(hash+salt) for each guess.
the salt only uses one operation per guess which isnt very noticable. The attacker still runs at full md5 speed meaning the salt provides no protection against a targeted brute force or dictionary attack.

The amount of cpu and gpu operations that're required to compute one hash. With the info we had earlier MD5 is over 4bil a second,
while bcrypt is only 3,387  a second which is a major difference because of how many iterations each one needs. Algorithm cost affects
the speed of each password crack.

Q10:

The developer is right that unique random salts beat a rainbow table. A precomputed table is useless when every password is hashed with
a different salt.

The vulneralbility tha still remains is that MD5 is still fast. At around 4 billion guesses per second indivial cracking is fast. The
estimated brute-force time would be 26^6 which would be 308,915,776 possible passwords. 308,915,776 ÷ 4,135,900,000 = 0.075.
Bcrypt would be 308,915,776 ÷ 3,387 = 91,208 seconds which would be 25 hours. The speed definitely matters in this case as a 6-letter
lowercase password stored as a salted md5 cracks in under a second regaurdless of what salt you use. While the same password stored as
 bcrypt takes over a day on the same hardware. 

Q12: I used argon2-cffi for my library because I read it was a better choice specifially for MD5 since it's designed for speed. It'd
preform as many operations as it per second which is what we wanted during this assignment (unless I wanted to sit here for a longer time).
From our q6 benchmark the M4 GPU computes MD5 at 4,135,900,000 hashes per second. Argon2 with the parameters I set - which would be 
(time_cost=2, memory_cost=65536, parallelism=2) runs around 3000 hashes a second. Around the same amount as bcrypt from our benchmark.
An attacker who steals the database can try 4 billion MD5 candidates per second but only a few thousand Argon2 candidates per second on
tje same hardware. Beyond raw speed Argon also has a good amount of memery (memory_cost=65536 = around 64 MB per hash) which also defeats
GPU acceleration because those devices have limited memore per core. While MD5 uses memory and runs at full GPU speed.

Q13: The two outputs would not be identical. They would be different strings every time even with the same input. This says that the library
generates a fresh random salt on every call and embeds it directly into the output string. The output encodes the algorithm (salt,hash,parameters)
all in one string without storing the salt seperately.

Q13/2: 

First hasher.verify(stored_hash, password) is called with the stored hash string and the plaintext password that was typed.

Second: Argon2 covers the stored hash string and takes out the embedded salt and the algorithm parameters from it.

Third: It re-calls the Argon2 function using that extracted salt and those parameters on the candidate password.

Fourth: It compares the result of the last step with our hash using a comparison that runs in constant time

Fifth: When they match verify returns true and the function returns True. If not verify raises Verifymismatcherror which would just return false

The plaintext is never stored because the verification only needs the hash and it will not recover the original input. Since hash
functions are one way you can check whether a candidate produces the ame output, but you cant reverse the hash to get back to the
original password. The database only ever stores the output of the hash not the input.

Q14: 1. Rainbow table attack - Against a plain MD5 storage an attacker could precompute a lookup table of MD5 password pairs and just
crack hashes without a GPU needed. This would fail against fixed_storage.py because Argon2 creates a unique random salt every hash and
automatically puts it within the output. The attacker would need a different hash table for every value which would be a lot storage wise.

2. Brute forcing and dictionary attacks - Reffering to part2 a dictionary attack against MD5 at 4.1 billion guesses per second cracked 
17/45 hashes from rockyou.txt in under a second but against fixed_storage.py the same attack would only run at 3000 guesses per second
due to Argon2's cost factor and memory requirements. This would make the same attack around 1.2 million times slower turning an attack
that would happen in under a second into weeks and possibly months on the same hardware.

 


